Modification (the only changes from upstream 18.1.3):
1. src/Lexer.ts: a fast path in the inline lexer (#tokenizeInlines) plus a
   findPlainChunkEnd helper. A run of plain inline text — no character that can
   start a non-text inline token (backslash, <, [, !, backtick, *, _, ~, &, @,
   www., ://, mailto:, xmpp:, or a hard break), with email-aware handling so
   an address containing `_` is still autolinked whole — is emitted directly
   as the single text token marked's inline lexer would produce, without
   running the tokenizers or the inline text regex. Every other run goes
   through marked's inline lexer unchanged. A run holding `@` takes no fast
   path at all: the email lookahead in the text rule can advance the loop one
   character at a time toward a `www.` the URL tokenizer then autolinks even
   mid-word, so a plain-text chunk in such a run is not provably plain.
2. src/Tokenizer.ts: the list tokenizer takes a line with indexOf + slice
   (takeFirstLine) instead of split('\n', 1)[0], avoiding an array allocation
   per line; and its inner loop skips the six block-start regex tests for a
   line whose first non-space character (within the item indent) cannot start
   any of them (fence, heading, html, blockquote, bullet, hr) — a plain
   continuation line, the common case.
3. (v2) Lexer.ts blockTokens: a paragraph fast path. A run of plain paragraph
   lines is scanned with a plain character loop (helpers Yt/Gt) and emitted
   directly as its paragraph token, skipping the block tokenizer chain; inside
   list items the same scan emits merged text tokens. The scan bails on any
   line that could start another block — headings, lists, fences, tables
   (delimiter rows start with |, -, or :), setext underlines (= or -) — and
   stops before blank lines; a trailing blank line at the end of a list item
   is left for the space tokenizer, which only touches the token's raw.
4. (v2) Lexer.ts blockTokens: a setext guard (helper Qt). The lheading regex
   only runs when some line up to the first blank line could be a setext
   underline; its heading text may span several lines, but it never crosses a
   blank line.
5. (v2) The HTML escape function is a regex-exec loop (slice + concat)
   instead of test + replace-with-callback: the fastest correct form measured
   locally (a charCodeAt loop measured slower than the regex in V8).
6. (v2) Tokenizer.ts list: the per-line regexes are plain character loops —
   the first non-space index (Jt), the tab-aware indent check (Xt), and the
   trailing double-blank-line check (Kt) — and the leading-whitespace replace
   is skipped when a line has no leading whitespace.
The changes are pure speedups: they cannot alter any output. (parse.js, the
entry's own module outside the vendored tree, additionally caches inline runs
per document: a fresh Map per parse() call, at most 2,000 entries, so no
result is ever reused between documents.)
